(function(a){const e=(t,n,i,s,l)=>({k:t.split(";"),t:n,a:i,s:(s||[]).map(o=>({t:o[0],d:o[1],href:o[2],btn:o[3]})),c:l||[]}),r=[e("linkedin profile;naukri profile;linkedin headline;linkedin about;naukri headline;naukri summary;profile writer;improve my linkedin;update naukri profile;linkedin and naukri","LinkedIn and Naukri profile writer","Open LinkedIn & Naukri in the menu. It writes your headline, About section, skills, messages and checklists from your resume. Tap Copy and paste on the site. Fill each [bracket] with a true fact.",[["LinkedIn and Naukri writer","Headline, About, skills, messages and checklists from your resume.","#/profilekit","Open"]],["Job openings","Resume tips"]),e("job openings;current openings;open positions;latest job openings;job vacancies;job openings page;openings matched to resume","Job openings matched to your resume","Open Job openings in the menu. It lists recent postings taken from companies own public job pages, with the posted date, and shows how well each one matches your resume. Your resume is read on your device only. Tap Apply to go to the company page and apply there. A job may be filled before our next check, so confirm on the company page. The list refreshes about every 3 hours and covers companies that publish an open job list.",[["Job openings","Recent openings with match percent.","#/openings","Open"],["Companies","Employers matched to your field.","#/companies","Open"]],["How to apply safely","Companies for me","Resume tips"]),e("resume tips;resume format;cv tips;how to write resume;improve resume;resume mistakes","Writing a strong resume","Keep it to 1 page (2 if you have 8+ years). Put a 2-line summary first, then skills, then experience with 3 to 5 bullets per role. Start bullets with action words and add a number to each: how many, how much, how fast. Match the tool names in the job post. Save as PDF, name it Firstname_Role.pdf, and remove photos, ID numbers and long paragraphs.",[["Check my resume","Get a score and fixes on your own text.","#/career/resume","Check resume"],["Make it better","Weak phrases replaced for you.","#/career/resume","Open"]],["What is ATS?","Fresher resume tips","LinkedIn profile"]),e("ats;applicant tracking;resume keywords;resume rejected;not getting calls;no interview calls","What is ATS and why resumes get ignored",'ATS software scans resumes for keywords from the job post and ranks them before a person reads. Use the same words as the job post (for example "incident management", not only "support"), use plain headings (Skills, Experience, Education), avoid tables and images for key content, and keep a simple one-column layout.',[["Compare with a job post","See which keywords you are missing.","#/career/match","Resume vs job"]],["Resume tips","Where to apply"]),e("fresher resume;fresher cv;no experience resume;student resume;first job resume","Resume for freshers","Put education and projects at the top. Describe 2 or 3 projects: what problem, what you built, tools used, result. Add internships, certificates, GitHub or portfolio, and 6 to 10 relevant skills. No experience is fine if you show proof of learning.",[["Fresher path","Pick your field and start.","#/start","Choose field"]],["Tell me about yourself","First job search"]),e("first job;fresher job;how to get first job;campus placement;get job as fresher;entry level job","Getting your first job","Pick one field and learn its basics for 4 to 8 weeks. Build 2 small projects, write a clean resume, apply daily on LinkedIn, Naukri and company career pages, ask for referrals from seniors, and practise the self-introduction aloud. Many freshers get calls after 30 to 60 applications, so keep a tracker.",[["Job search guide","Eight stages from resume to offer.","#/journey","Open guide"],["Track applications","Never miss a follow-up.","#/tracker","Open tracker"]],["Where to apply","Interview preparation","Internship"]),e("job without experience;no experience job;job with no experience;zero experience;how to get a job without experience;experience required","Getting a job without experience","Employers want proof, not only years. Pick one field, learn its basics for 4 to 8 weeks, build two small projects, and show them on your resume. Try internships, trainee and apprentice roles, contract work and referrals. Apply to many, and practise your self introduction.",[["Choose a field","Pick one field to focus on.","#/start","Choose field"],["Study path","A step by step path for beginners.","#/learn","Open learning"]],["Resume for freshers","Where to find jobs","Tell me about yourself"]),e("python;learn python;java;learn coding;learn programming;start coding;coding for beginners","Learning to code","Pick one language (Python is common for beginners), practise 45 minutes daily, and build small programs such as a calculator or expense tracker. Then learn Git and basic SQL. Put 2 projects on GitHub. Use free courses and practise problems on the Programming topics here.",[["Programming topics","Basics, logic and projects.","#/learn/programming","Open programming"]],["Getting your first job","Resume for freshers"]),e("salary revision;salary hike;ask for raise;increment;appraisal mail;hike mail;salary review mail;raise email;ask hike","Asking for a salary revision","Ask in person first, then send a short email as a record. Bring 2 or 3 results with numbers (time saved, tickets closed, money earned), say what new work you took on, and quote the market range for your role. Ask for a review meeting, not an ultimatum. If the answer is 'later', ask what you must show by then and write it down. Never compare with a colleague.",[["Salary revision email draft","Open the ready draft, edit it and copy.","#/toolkit/mail","Email drafts"]],["Promotion email","Reply to a job offer","How to explain my work"]),e("promotion;ask promotion;next level;get promoted;promotion mail;bigger role","Asking for a promotion","Show that you already do the next level's work for several months. Ask your manager what is missing and agree on a 90 day plan. Promotion follows the review cycle, so ask about timing. Keep a list of your wins with numbers.",[["Promotion email draft","Open the ready draft, edit it and copy.","#/toolkit/mail","Email drafts"]],["Salary revision email","How to explain my work"]),e("explain professionally;professional way;speak professionally;how to explain in interview;explain project;explain my work;communicate in interview;answer structure;how to answer","Explaining your work like a professional","Use Headline, Detail, Impact: answer in one sentence, add two or three facts, end with the result and a number. For 'tell me about a time' questions use STAR. Say 'I' for your own work and credit the team separately. Seniors should talk about decisions and trade-offs, not only tasks.",[["How to explain my work","Frameworks with examples.","#/toolkit/pro","Open"]],["Project story","STAR method","Tell me about yourself"]),e("project story;describe project;tell me about your project;explain my project;project explanation","Describing your project in 60 seconds","Say why (the problem), what (your role and tools), how (the two hardest steps) and the result (a number and a lesson). Keep it under a minute and practise it aloud. Interviewers then ask follow-ups from what you said, so only mention what you can explain.",[["How to explain my work","Frameworks with examples.","#/toolkit/pro","Open"]],["How to explain my work","STAR method"]),e("star method;star format;behavioural answer;situation task action result;tell me about a time","The STAR method","Situation (one line), Task (your goal), Action (what YOU did), Result (a number or a lesson). Spend about 15 percent on situation and task, 60 percent on action, 25 percent on result. Prepare 5 stories that you can bend to many questions: a conflict, a mistake, a win, a deadline, a leadership moment.",[["How to explain my work","Frameworks with examples.","#/toolkit/pro","Open"]],["How to explain my work","Tell me about yourself"]),e("technical to non technical;explain simply;explain to manager;explain to client;simple language;analogy","Explaining technical ideas simply","Start with the outcome, use one everyday comparison (a security guard for a firewall, a post office for a router), avoid short forms, and check: 'Does that make sense?'. If you can teach it to a friend, you can explain it in an interview.",[["How to explain my work","Frameworks with examples.","#/toolkit/pro","Open"]],["How to explain my work","Project story"]),e("offer negotiation email;negotiate offer mail;reply to offer;counter offer mail;negotiate email","Replying to a job offer","Thank them first, then ask: 'Based on my experience with [skills] and [result], I was expecting [range]. Is there flexibility?' Ask for fixed, variable, joining bonus and role level in writing. Do not lie about your current pay or other offers.",[["Offer reply draft","Open the ready draft, edit it and copy.","#/toolkit/mail","Email drafts"]],["Salary negotiation","Accept offer email","Decline offer email"]),e("follow up email;interview follow up;thank you email;thank you mail;no reply after interview;recruiter not replying","Following up after an interview","Send a thank-you note within 24 hours mentioning one specific thing from the interview. If there is no reply, wait 5 to 7 working days, send one polite follow up, and a second after another week. Then move on and keep applying.",[["Follow up email draft","Open the ready draft, edit it and copy.","#/toolkit/mail","Email drafts"]],["Where to apply","Referral message"]),e("resignation letter;resign mail;resignation email;how to resign;resignation draft","Writing a resignation","Hold the signed offer first. Tell your manager in person, then send a short thankful resignation email to your manager and HR asking them to confirm your last day. Do not criticise anyone. Offer a handover plan.",[["Job search guide","Offer to joining and notice period.","#/journey","Open"],["Notice period email draft","Open the ready draft, edit it and copy.","#/toolkit/mail","Email drafts"]],["Notice period","Counter offer","Notice period"]),e("counter offer;stay offer;company offered more;manager offered hike to stay","Handling a counter offer","Ask why you wanted to leave. Money rarely fixes growth or manager problems. If you stay, get the new terms in writing first. Whichever way you go, stay polite and finish the handover.",[["Counter offer reply draft","Open the ready draft, edit it and copy.","#/toolkit/mail","Email drafts"]],["Salary negotiation","Resignation letter"]),e("work from home request;wfh request;request wfh;ask for wfh;ask wfh;flexible work;hybrid request;remote work request","Asking for work from home","Offer a one month trial with clear availability, a daily update and how you will keep deadlines. Focus on how work gets delivered, not on personal reasons.",[["Flexible work email draft","Open the ready draft, edit it and copy.","#/toolkit/mail","Email drafts"]],["Salary revision","Promotion"]),e("reference;reference request;ask for reference;referral message to employee","Asking for references and referrals","Ask people before listing them, and send them your resume and the job description. For a referral, send a four line message with the exact job link, one matching result and the resume attached. Thank them, whatever happens.",[["Reference and referral drafts","Open the ready draft, edit it and copy.","#/toolkit/mail","Email drafts"]],["Referral","Follow up email"]),e("rejected;rejection;not selected;feedback after rejection;failed interview","Dealing with a rejection","Thank them, ask once for feedback, write down what you could improve and practise that question. Most rejections are about fit or timing. Apply to many roles and keep a tracker.",[["Feedback request draft","Open the ready draft, edit it and copy.","#/toolkit/mail","Email drafts"]],["Interview preparation","Tell me about yourself"]),e("senior interview;experienced interview;lead interview;manager interview;leadership interview;10 years experience interview","Interviews for experienced and senior people","You are judged on decisions, trade-offs, results for the business and how you grow others. Prepare 5 stories: a hard decision, a conflict, a failure, a turnaround and a team you built. Use numbers and say what you would do differently.",[["How to explain my work","Frameworks with examples.","#/toolkit/pro","Open"]],["Senior interview","How to explain my work"]),e("mock interview feedback;improve confidence;nervous interview;interview fear;interview anxiety","Feeling nervous before an interview","Nerves are normal. Practise your first answer until it is smooth, rehearse out loud twice, sleep well, arrive early and breathe slowly before you start. Practising here counts: every answered question helps.",[["Practise answers","Speak and get feedback.","#/interview","Practise"]],["Tell me about yourself","How to explain my work"]),e("companies hiring;which companies;top companies;companies for freshers;companies in india;company list;best company;where to work;mnc companies;product companies;service companies;companies in chennai;companies in bangalore;companies in hyderabad;companies in pune","Which companies should I target?","Aim for a mix: 5 dream companies, 10 realistic ones and 10 safe ones. Service firms hire freshers in large numbers, product and global centres hire fewer and prefer experience or referrals, startups move fast, banks and public sector often hire through exams. Open the Companies page: it lists nearly 300 employers matched to your field, experience, tech or non-tech and city. Always check each careers page for current openings.",[["Companies for me","Matched to your field, experience and city.","#/companies","Open companies"],["Referral email","Ask an employee for a referral.","#/toolkit/mail","Email drafts"]],["Where to apply","Referral","Getting your first job"]),e("where to apply;job portals;job sites;how to apply for jobs;find jobs;job search;naukri;linkedin jobs","Where to find jobs","Use LinkedIn Jobs, Naukri, Indeed, Foundit and company career pages. Turn on job alerts, apply within 3 days of posting, and message the recruiter or an employee with a short note. Referrals get far more replies than cold applications. Beware of any job that asks you to pay money.",[["Where and when to apply","Portals, timing and referral steps.","#/journey/5","Open"],["Roles for my level","Search links for your field.","#/jobs","See roles"]],["Fake job offers","Referral message"]),e("fake job;job scam;pay for job;registration fee;job fraud;offer letter fake","Spotting fake job offers","Real employers never ask you to pay for a job, training kit or documents. Be careful with offers on WhatsApp or Telegram only, Gmail or Yahoo company addresses, instant offers with no interview, and urgent money requests. Check the company on its official site and LinkedIn, and call its official number.",[],["Where to apply","Offer letter checklist"]),e("referral;ask referral;linkedin message;cold message;reach out recruiter;networking","Asking for a referral",'Message in 4 lines: who you are, the exact role link, one skill that matches, and a polite ask. Example: "Hi Priya, I saw the ServiceNow Developer opening (link). I have 3 years in Business Rules and Flow Designer. Could you refer me or point me to the right person? Resume attached." Thank them whether or not they can help.',[["Job search guide","Referral steps in stage 5.","#/journey/5","Open"]],["LinkedIn profile","Resume tips"]),e("tell me about yourself;self introduction;introduce yourself;self intro",'Answering "Tell me about yourself"',"Use present, past, future in about 60 seconds. Present: your role or studies and main skills. Past: one or two highlights with a result. Future: what you want next and why this role fits. Do not read your resume line by line or share family and personal details.",[["Practise it aloud","Speak and get feedback.","#/speak/intro","Speaking lab"],["See the model answer","HR question with guidance.","#/interview/hr","HR questions"]],["Strengths and weaknesses","Why should we hire you"]),e("interview preparation;prepare for interview;how to prepare interview;interview tips;interview checklist","How to prepare for an interview","Day before: read the job post, revise your resume line by line, prepare 5 stories (success, failure, conflict, leadership, pressure), practise the self-introduction aloud, and prepare 3 questions for them. Interview day: join 10 minutes early, test camera and mic, keep a notebook, and listen fully before answering.",[["Interview home","Questions, mock and rounds.","#/interview","Open"],["Mock interview","Built from your resume.","#/mock","Start mock"]],["STAR method","Online interview tips"]),e("star method;star technique;behavioural answer;behavioral interview;situation task action result","The STAR method",'Situation: the context in one line. Task: your responsibility. Action: what you did, step by step, using "I". Result: the outcome with a number, plus what you learned. Keep it to about 90 seconds and pick stories where you made the difference.',[["Practise behavioural questions","Answer and get scored.","#/interview/hr/Behavioural","Practise"]],["Difficult manager","Strengths and weaknesses"]),e("online interview tips;video interview tips;online interview;video interview;zoom interview;teams interview;virtual interview;interview camera","Online interview tips",'Test internet, camera, mic and link 15 minutes early. Sit facing a window, keep a plain background, look at the camera when you speak, mute notifications, keep your resume and a glass of water nearby, and say "Could you repeat that?" if the audio breaks.',[],["Interview preparation","Dress code"]),e("dress code;what to wear;interview dress;formal dress;interview outfit","What to wear to an interview","Dress one level smarter than the company daily wear. For most corporate and IT roles: ironed formal shirt and trousers or a simple formal outfit, clean shoes, minimal accessories. Core and site roles: neat clothes and safety shoes if asked. Comfortable and clean beats expensive.",[],["Interview preparation"]),e("salary negotiation;salary tips;negotiate salary;how to negotiate;ask for more salary;counter offer;negotiation script","Negotiating salary","Research the range for the role, city and your level, then speak after you have the offer in writing. Say thanks, show enthusiasm, give your number as a range backed by your value, and ask about the full package, not only fixed pay. Never lie about your current salary and never accept on the call; ask for a day.",[["Negotiation scripts","Words to say and letters to send.","#/journey/7","Open stage 7"]],["Notice period","Compare two offers"]),e("expected salary;salary expectation;current ctc;expected ctc;what salary should i ask;ctc","Answering expected salary",'Say you are flexible for the right role, then give a researched range: "Based on my skills and the market for this role, I am looking at X to Y." State your current CTC in components if asked, honestly. A range gives room to negotiate.',[],["Salary negotiation","Compare offers"]),e("compare offers;two offers;which offer;offer comparison;take home salary;in hand salary","Comparing two job offers","Compare fixed pay, variable, joining bonus, benefits and take-home, then growth, learning, manager, work mode, commute, company stability and notice or bond terms. A bigger number is not always better; compare the second-year picture too.",[["Offer comparison tool","Enter both offers side by side.","#/journey/7","Compare"]],["Salary negotiation"]),e("notice period;serving notice;resign;resignation letter;buyout;early release;last working day","Notice period and resigning","Read your contract for the notice clause. Resign in writing, keep the tone polite, hand over work properly, and ask your manager about early release or buyout if the new employer needs you sooner. Do not leave without relieving and experience letters; they matter in background checks.",[["Resignation letters","Templates for resign, early exit and decline.","#/journey/7","Open"]],["Counter offer","Background verification"]),e("counter offer;current company counter;stay or leave;should i accept counter offer","When your company makes a counter offer","Ask why the raise came only after you resigned. Think about the reasons you wanted to leave: pay alone rarely fixes growth, manager or work issues. If you stay, get it in writing; if you leave, stay thankful and keep the door open.",[],["Notice period"]),e("background verification;bgv;reference check;employment verification;relieving letter;experience letter","Background verification","Employers check identity, education, past employment, and sometimes address and criminal records. Keep payslips, relieving and experience letters, degree certificates and ID ready, and make sure dates and titles on your resume match them exactly.",[],["Offer letter checklist"]),e("offer letter;check offer letter;joining;appointment letter;probation;bond;service agreement","Checking an offer letter","Check designation, salary breakup, variable and when it is paid, joining date, probation length, notice period during and after probation, bond or training cost clauses, work location and mode, and benefits. Ask HR to clarify anything vague in writing before you resign.",[["Offer checklist","Red flags and questions to ask.","#/journey/7","Open"]],["Bond clause","Notice period"]),e("first 90 days;first day at job;new job tips;onboarding tips;probation tips","Your first 90 days","Days 1 to 30: learn the people, tools and expectations; ask questions. Days 31 to 60: take ownership of a small task and deliver it well. Days 61 to 90: share one improvement and confirm goals with your manager. Keep notes, be on time and be easy to work with.",[["First 90 days plan","Week by week for your level.","#/journey/8","Open"]],["Probation"]),e("career gap;gap in resume;employment gap;break in career;maternity break;return to work","Explaining a career gap","Give a short honest reason (family, health, study, relocation), what you did to stay current (course, project, freelance, volunteering) and that you are ready now. Do not apologise or over-explain. List the gap by years on the resume, not hidden.",[["Practise this answer","HR question with guidance.","#/interview/hr","Practise"]],["Return to work"]),e("switch career;change field;career change;move to it;non it to it;switch domain;career switch","Switching careers",'List skills that transfer, pick a target role, learn the basics in 8 to 12 weeks, build 2 proof projects, rewrite your resume with a headline like "Support lead moving to ServiceNow", and target trainee or associate roles at service companies. Expect to start a level lower and rise faster than a fresher.',[["Switch careers plan","Plan for your move.","#/switch","Open"]],["Resume tips","Starting from zero"]),e("which field;choose career;confused career;what to learn;best career;which course;career guidance","Choosing a field","Look at what you enjoy doing for hours, what you are good at, and what hires in your city. Try a 1-week sample of two fields before you commit. Our field list shows topics, roles and interview rounds for each.",[["Choose your field","See all fields.","#/start","Choose field"]],["First job","Learning path"]),e("starting from zero;learn from scratch;how to start learning;learning path;study plan;roadmap;beginner","Starting from zero","Pick one field. Follow its study path in order: lesson, scenario, quiz. Study 45 minutes a day, practise aloud, and build a tiny project every 2 weeks. Review mistakes on weekends.",[["Study paths","Pick a path for your goal.","#/paths","Open paths"]],["Daily study plan"]),e("daily study plan;study schedule;time table;how many hours;consistency","A daily study plan that works","30 minutes new topic, 15 minutes quiz or scenario, 15 minutes speaking aloud. Same time every day, and one rest day a week. A small daily habit beats long irregular sessions.",[],["Starting from zero"]),e("english speaking;improve english;spoken english;communication skills;fluency;fear of speaking","Improving spoken English","Speak aloud for 10 minutes daily: read a paragraph, then answer one interview question in your own words. Record yourself, count filler words, and fix one issue per week. Learn phrases, not single words. Fluency comes from practice, not perfect grammar.",[["Speaking lab","Practise and see a coach report.","#/speak","Open lab"]],["Self-introduction"]),e("group discussion;gd tips;gd topics;group discussion interview","Group discussion tips","Listen first, start only if you have a clear point, speak in short points with one example, build on others by name, stay calm in disagreement, and summarise if the group stalls. Quality and respect matter more than speaking most.",[],["Interview preparation"]),e("aptitude;aptitude test;reasoning;quantitative;written test;online test;assessment","Aptitude and online tests","Practise percentages, ratios, time and work, number series, and basic reading. Do timed sets, skip hard questions first, and review wrong ones. For technical tests, practise on your own machine without help.",[],["Interview preparation"]),e("hr round;hr interview;hr questions;hr round questions","The HR round","HR checks attitude, stability, expectations and fit: why this company, strengths, notice period, salary, relocation and shifts. Be honest, calm and positive. Never criticise past employers.",[["HR questions","Over 100 with model answers.","#/interview/hr","Open"]],["Salary expectation","Notice period"]),e("strengths and weaknesses;weakness answer;greatest weakness;strength answer","Strengths and weaknesses",'Pick two real strengths with proof. For a weakness, choose a real but small one, show what you do to improve it, and give progress. Avoid fake weaknesses like "I work too hard".',[["Practise it","HR question with guidance.","#/interview/hr","Practise"]],["Tell me about yourself"]),e("why should we hire you;why hire me;why you","Why should we hire you?","Do not compare with others. Say your top two skills for this role, one result with a number, and how you will help the team quickly. End with enthusiasm for the work.",[],["Strengths and weaknesses"]),e("difficult manager;conflict with manager;difficult boss;bad manager;toxic workplace;toxic team;workplace stress","Handling a difficult manager or team","Document facts, speak privately and specifically about the work, propose a solution, and involve HR only if talking does not work or the issue is serious. In interviews, describe conflicts neutrally and focus on what you did.",[],["STAR method"]),e("burnout;work stress;overworked;work life balance;mental health at work","Work stress and balance","Plan the week, protect sleep and breaks, say what you can deliver, and speak early when workload is too high. If stress is affecting your health, talk to someone you trust or a professional.",[],["Time management"]),e("time management;productivity;prioritise;pomodoro;procrastination","Managing time","List the top three tasks each morning, block focus time, silence notifications, and review at day end. Break big tasks into 30-minute steps.",[],["Work stress"]),e("linkedin profile;linkedin tips;linkedin headline;linkedin summary","A good LinkedIn profile",'Use a clear photo, a headline with role and skills (not only "Looking for opportunities"), a short About section, skills, and projects. Turn on "Open to work" for recruiters only, post occasionally, and connect with people in your field.',[],["Referral message"]),e("projects that impress;github;portfolio;projects for resume;project ideas;portfolio website;side project","Projects that impress","Pick problems you understand, build small and finish. Add a readme with problem, steps and screenshots, and one clear result. Two finished projects beat ten half-done ones.",[["Project interview","Questions on your own projects.","#/project","Open"]],["Fresher resume"]),e("certifications worth it;certification;certificate;which certification;course certificate;is certification worth","Are certifications worth it?","They help most for freshers and switchers, and for tools with official exams like cloud, ServiceNow, Salesforce or Security+. Pair each with a project. A certificate with no practice rarely wins interviews.",[],["Starting from zero"]),e("remote job;work from home;wfh;hybrid;remote work","Finding remote work",'Search "remote" and "hybrid" filters, show clear written communication, keep a strong portfolio, and be ready for async interviews. Beware of remote jobs that ask for payment.',[],["Fake job offers"]),e("freelance;freelancing;side income;upwork;fiverr","Freelancing basics","Start with one clear service, 3 sample works, a simple price list and a profile on a freelance platform. Deliver small jobs well for reviews. Keep a contract and invoice for each client.",[],["Projects that impress"]),e("internship;intern;stipend;summer internship;how to get internship","Getting an internship","Apply on Internshala, LinkedIn and company sites, show one or two projects, write a short cover note and follow up after a week. Prefer roles with real work and a mentor.",[],["First job"]),e("appraisal;promotion;get promoted;raise;hike;how to get hike","Getting a raise or promotion","Keep a list of results with numbers, ask for goals early in the year, take on bigger work, and ask for a review meeting with a clear case. If growth is blocked for long, look outside.",[["Career levels","See the next level in your field.","#/ladder","Open"]],["Salary negotiation"]),e("government job;sarkari;upsc;ssc;bank exam;railway exam;psu","Government job basics","Check official notifications on the department website, prepare the syllabus and past papers, and apply before the deadline with correct documents. Use only official sites and never pay anyone for a seat.",[],["Fake job offers"]),e("study abroad;job abroad;visa;overseas job;migrate","Jobs abroad","Check official embassy and company sites, apply through legitimate portals and never pay agents for guaranteed jobs. Confirm job offers with the employer directly and read visa rules on government sites.",[],["Fake job offers"]),e("how does padimachi work;what is padimachi;about padimachi;who made padimachi;is it free","About Padimachi","Padimachi is a free career buddy for working people and freshers in India: upload a resume for a career report, learn your field, practise interview questions out loud, track applications and plan offers. Your resume and answers stay on your device.",[["How it works","Six simple steps.","#/guide","Open"]],["First job","Resume tips"]),e("privacy;is my data safe;resume safe;data stored;do you save my resume","Is my data safe?","Your resume and answers stay in your own browser and are not uploaded. The site only counts visits anonymously. You can remove your resume or reset everything from the Profile page.",[["Privacy page","Plain language details.","privacy.html","Read"]],[]),e("best month to change job;best time to change job;when to change job;which month good for job change;when should i switch job;job change month;good time to switch","Best months to change jobs in India","Hiring is busiest in January to March (new budgets, bonus payouts) and July to September (after appraisals). October and November are steady. December is slowest, so use it to prepare. Start learning about 3 to 4 months before a busy window, and start applying about 2 months before you want to leave, since notice periods are 1 to 3 months. If a bonus or appraisal is close, joining after it is a smart move.",[["Career mentor","See your own timeline with a month chart.","#/mentor","Open mentor"],["Notice period","Plan your exit.","#/toolkit","Toolkit"]],["Non IT to IT switch","How long to get a job after switching","Package for switchers"]),e("non it to it;non it to it switch;move to it;switch to it;change to it field;jump to it;nonit to it;career switch;change career;switch career;new field;new stream;change stream","Moving from non IT to IT (or any new field)","Very possible, and many people do it. Pick one IT role that fits your background (for example support or testing for service work, data or ServiceNow if you like logic). Learn the basics for 3 to 5 months with 1 to 2 hours a day, build two small projects, rewrite your resume to show skills that carry over, and apply to trainee and associate roles. The Career mentor shows the time, ballpark pay, best months and a monthly plan for your exact move.",[["Career mentor","Time, pay, months and checklist.","#/mentor","Open mentor"],["Switch plan","90 day plan and introduction.","#/switch","Switch plan"]],["Best month to change job","How long to get a job after switching","Package for switchers"]),e("how long to get a job after switching;how long does it take to switch;time to get job;how many months to get job;how long will it take to get a job;how long to switch career;how long job search","How long does it take to get a job after switching?","Most people need about 2 to 5 months to learn the basics and 2 to 4 months to search, so roughly 4 to 9 months in total. Closer fields and more daily study time make it shorter. Senior roles can take 4 to 6 months of searching, so start early and use referrals.",[["Career mentor","Your personal timeline.","#/mentor","Open mentor"]],["Best month to change job","Package for switchers","Ways to find a job"]),e("package for switchers;salary after switching;salary for fresher it;starting salary after switch;what package will i get;market value;current market value;initial package;entry salary;salary for career switch","Starting package after a switch","In India a switcher or fresher usually starts around 2.5 to 8 lakh a year depending on the field, city and company. Tech fields such as data, cloud, programming and ServiceNow sit at the higher end, while support, teaching and core roles sit nearer 2.2 to 5 lakh. With experience in a close field the offer is often near your current pay. Many people catch up in 12 to 18 months. These are ballpark numbers, so check AmbitionBox and Naukri for the latest.",[["Career mentor","See the range for your field and level.","#/mentor","Open mentor"],["Salary talk","Draft emails for salary and offers.","#/toolkit","Toolkit"]],["Best month to change job","Ways to find a job","Salary negotiation"]),e("ways to find a job;different ways to search job;how to search for jobs;job search methods;where to search jobs;how to find job;channels to find job","Ways to find a job","Use several at the same time: Naukri and LinkedIn (set alerts and keep your profile fresh), company careers pages, referrals (message people for advice, it is the strongest route), consultants (free for you, never pay), Instahyre, Cutshort and Wellfound for tech and startups, walk-in drives and trainee batches, and freelance or internship work to build proof. Apply early in the day and follow up after two weeks.",[["Career mentor","Channels matched to your field.","#/mentor","Open mentor"],["Application tracker","Keep every application in one place.","#/tracker","Tracker"]],["Best month to change job","How long to get a job after switching"]),e("when will job openings come;when openings come;when do companies hire;hiring season;hiring months;when to apply;which company should i try;target companies","When do openings come?","Roles open when teams get budget: mostly January to March and July to September. Entry and trainee roles appear all year. Senior roles are fewer and often come by referral, so start 4 to 6 months early. Pick 5 to 10 target companies, follow their careers pages, set alerts and ask for referrals. The Career mentor shows how each company type hires.",[["Career mentor","Add your target companies.","#/mentor","Open mentor"],["Companies","Employers matched to you.","#/companies","Companies"]],["Best month to change job","Ways to find a job"]),e("what changes after switching;environment change;new work culture;will it be hard to adjust;how to adapt new job;settle in new field;adapt to new role","Settling into a new field","Expect new tools, new words and a learning curve for the first 60 to 90 days, then it feels normal. Ask for a buddy in week one, write what you learn each day, ask short clear questions and share small wins early. Your old field gives you strengths such as customer handling, discipline or domain knowledge, so mention them proudly.",[["Career mentor","What changes, field by field.","#/mentor","Open mentor"]],["Non IT to IT switch","First 90 days"]),e("why are you leaving;why leaving your job;why leave your job;reason for leaving;why do you want to leave;why are you leaving your job","Why are you leaving your current job?",'Give a positive, honest reason: growth, learning or scope, not complaints. Say what you achieved there, what you want next and why this role gives it. Never blame your manager or company. Example: "I have learned a lot and delivered results, and now I want bigger responsibility in this area."',[["HR questions","Practise this answer aloud.","#/interview/hr","Practise"]],["Salary negotiation","Tell me about yourself"])];a.FAQ=r})(typeof window!="undefined"?window:globalThis);
